{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And you shall designate for yourselves cities.</b> From the word קריה (kirya, city)." "<b>And the one who strikes a soul in error shall flee there.</b> But he is not liable to death, since he did not kill with intent. And this judgment teaches that the heart is the essence of the person and the essence of all the commandments and all the transgressions; therefore he is punished with exile and not with death, because his heart was not in agreement with the murder, and the liability of the death penalty does not apply until the body and the heart are partners in that murder — the body in its movement and the heart in its intention. And since the two did not agree in this, he is exiled and is not put to death. And likewise regarding the commandments, everything depends on the heart: if one performed a commandment but did not intend in his heart to do it for the sake of Heaven, he has no reward for it, for a person must intend in the commandments to perform them for the sake of Heaven. And so David, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 119:48): ״ואשא כפי אל מצותיך אשר אהבתי״ — and the lifting of hands is the intention, in the manner of what is written (Lamentations 3:41): ״נשא לבבנו אל כפים״." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: ״מכה נפש בשגגה״ — specifically in error, and not with malice aforethought. For if he killed with malice aforethought and said, 'I killed in error,' they do not grant him asylum, and even if he entered the altar they remove him from there and put him to death, as it is said (Exodus 21:14): ״וכי יזיד איש על רעהו להרגו בערמה מעם מזבחי תקחנו למות״. For so we find with Joab, of whom it is written (I Kings 2:28): ״ויחזק בקרנות המזבח״, and from there they took him and Benaiah killed him. And it is a law of the Torah that one who kills in error is exiled, for so we find with the first Adam. This is what Scripture says (Psalms 25:6): ״זכור רחמיך ה' וחסדיך כי מעולם המה״. David said: Master of the universe, Your mercies and Your kindnesses are upon the murderer, for You prepare for him cities of refuge and he is exiled there, since he did not kill with intent and is not worthy of death. And those mercies have been from of old, for so You did to the first Adam, who brought death to all generations, and You had mercy upon him to be exiled, as it is said (Genesis 3:24): ״וישלחהו ה' אלהים מגן עדן״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ויגרש את האדם״. Therefore it says: ״זכור רחמיך ה' וחסדיך כי מעולם המה״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:38:02.011826798Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}